WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.317
Sometimes we want one status that summarizes the first problem found in a record.

00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:09.522
This product-validation query checks several rules in order.

00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:16.722
It looks for a missing product name, a missing price, an invalid price, and a negative stock quantity.

00:00:16.722 --> 00:00:21.506
If none of those conditions is triggered, the row passes the selected rules.

00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:28.500
The order matters because a case expression returns the first matching condition.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.380
For example, if a product has both a missing name and a negative price,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.140
this particular query reports the missing name first.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.080
That does not mean the price problem disappeared.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:48.395
For a full data-quality system, we may want separate issue flags so multiple problems can be recorded at the same time.

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:55.145
Use this example to understand row-level validation, but also recognize its limitation:

00:00:55.145 --> 00:00:59.296
one status may summarize rather than capture every issue.
